  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that $g(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[-4,-1]$ and differentiable on  $(-4,-1).$\n\nGiven that $g(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following: \n\n> *There exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(c)=\\frac{g(b)-g(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-4$ and $b=-1.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{g(-1)-g(-4)}{-1-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-(-1)}{-1+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n>  *The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-4,-1)$ such that ${g}^{′}(c)=\\frac{2}{3}.$*",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
